  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms Tuesday\nwith a strong frontal passage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe can't rule out additional showers or a thunderstorm or two in\nany one portion of our forecast region the remainder of the\nevening. Previous convection is weakening or has weakened. \nThere remains about 1000 J/kg of ML CAPE and 20 to 30 knots of \nwind shear over a large portion of our region. Therefore, \ninstability and shear is present to support additional \nconvection the remainder of the evening. \n\nAs for after midnight into daybreak Tuesday, models are diverse\nin a solution that a cluster of showers and strong thunderstorms\nor multiple individual thunderstorms could develop well to the\nnorthwest and move into northwestern and north-central zones\nlater tonight toward the early morning rush. There is an\nincreased chance that we will see scattered areas of severe\nthunderstorms in our forecast region Tuesday afternoon into the\nevening. The main timeframe of concern appears to be 2pm to 8pm.\nWe could see multiple linear segments of thunderstorms or a\ncluster that materializes, spreads outward, and generates\nadditional strong to severe thunderstorms. All of this relies on\na surface low pressure system that will drop down from the lower\nGreat Lakes and Midwest and push across the Appalachians during\nthe peak heating of the day. The main threats Tuesday afternoon\ninto Tuesday evening will be damaging winds and heavy downpours\nthat could lead to instances of flash flooding. We could also\nencounter large hail as tops of severe thunderstorm could poke\nseveral thousand feet upwards before collapsing. The Storms\nPrediction Center has most of our region in a Slight Risk for\nSevere Thunderstorms. It is around mid-evening that a cold front\nis expected to push through the region and bring slightly cooler\nand drier air into the region the remainder of Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few chances for rain through the weekend,\nbut no hazardous weather expected between Wednesday and Sunday \nat this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe upper-low over the northeast CONUS will linger through Friday \nbefore finally pulling off to the northeast. As a result, there will \nbe non-zero rain chances each day, primarily areas further \nnorth and east. Friday could be dry for all, but still a bit of \nuncertainty regarding exactly when the upper low departs. \n\nA broad upper-level trough will begin to form over the Ohio River \nValley over the weekend. As this trough tracks east, cloud cover & \nprecipitation chances will begin to increase, particularly between \nSaturday evening and Sunday as the low tracks offshore and towards \nthe northeast. Severe probabilities look low at this time, with a \npretty weak wind field aloft. Nonetheless, weekend showers are \npossible with this system should the synoptic setup continue to \ntrend toward development over the Midwest.",
